<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><span class="status-pill completed">Completed</span><div class="eyebrow">Backend · FastAPI · PostgreSQL · Stripe</div><h1>FlyRank Usage Metering & Billing Engine</h1><p class="section-lead">A backend capstone for tenant-scoped API and AI-token metering, plan quotas, Stripe test-mode subscription synchronization, cost estimation and monthly usage reconciliation.</p></div></section>

<section><div class="container content">
<section class="story-section"><h2>Project at a glance</h2><div class="story-grid"><div class="story-step"><strong>1 · Objective</strong><p>Provide reliable tenant-level usage tracking and subscription access for a usage-based API product without double-counting requests.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>2 · Data</strong><p>Simulated API and AI-token usage records, tenant accounts and Stripe test-mode subscription events.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>3 · Method</strong><p>Build a FastAPI/PostgreSQL service for metering, quotas, cost estimation, idempotent usage recording and verified Stripe entitlement synchronisation.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>4 · Evaluation</strong><p>Exercise duplicate requests, exact quota boundaries, invalid and repeated Stripe webhooks, durable usage records and monthly reconciliation.</p></div><div class="story-step story-impact"><strong>5 · Outcome</strong><p>The service demonstrates the controls needed to reduce usage-recording, subscription-state and billing-logic errors before real commercial billing is introduced.</p></div></div></section>
<h2>Context and objective</h2>
<p>A multi-tenant AI product needs more than a usage counter. It needs to know which tenant consumed what, prevent accidental double counting, enforce plan limits consistently, synchronize paid entitlements safely, and make usage auditable enough to reconcile later.</p>

<h2>Data and constraints</h2>
<p>The project uses simulated API and AI-token usage and <strong>Stripe test mode only</strong>. It does not call a real AI provider and does not create real invoices, tax calculations, charges or overage bills.</p>

<h2>Technical solution</h2>
<h3>System design</h3>
<ul>
<li>FastAPI service with SQLAlchemy-backed persistence and PostgreSQL as the target database.</li>
<li>Two seeded plans: Free at 1,000 API calls and 100,000 AI tokens per UTC month; Pro at 10,000 calls and 1,000,000 tokens.</li>
<li>Tenant-bound HMAC proofs on tenant-scoped public endpoints.</li>
<li>Exactly-once usage recording through a database uniqueness constraint on tenant plus idempotency key.</li>
<li>Independent API-call and AI-token quota enforcement with exact-limit requests accepted.</li>
<li>Current-period usage summaries with remaining allowance and integer-cent AI cost estimates.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Stripe entitlement flow</h3>
<p>Creating a Checkout Session does not grant Pro access locally. Entitlements change only after verified subscription webhooks are received and processed.</p>
<ul>
<li>Raw request bodies are verified against <code>Stripe-Signature</code> before database work.</li>
<li>Supported subscription events grant Pro only for an active subscription containing the configured Pro Price and restore Free access on deletion.</li>
<li>Stripe event IDs are globally deduplicated so valid webhook retries are idempotent.</li>
<li>Forged, stale, malformed or wrong-secret events are rejected without local state mutation.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout">Checkout initiation is not an entitlement grant. Verified subscription state is the authority.</div>
<h3>Metering and reconciliation</h3>
<p>AI usage is categorized as input, cached input, output or reasoning tokens. Estimated cost uses integer arithmetic and one final half-up rounding step. A separately runnable rollup job derives each tenant/month summary from the same source-event logic used by the live usage endpoint, with idempotent reruns and retries for transient database failures.</p>

<h2>Evaluation and evidence</h2>
<p>The project includes deterministic tests and a repeatable demo rehearsal covering near-quota refusal, idempotent retries, durable usage rows, signed Stripe Free-to-Pro synchronization, forged and duplicate webhook handling, exact usage summaries and monthly reconciliation.</p>

<h2>Key results</h2>
<ul>
<li>Tenant + idempotency-key uniqueness prevents duplicate usage recording.</li>
<li>API-call and AI-token quotas are enforced independently, including exact-limit requests.</li>
<li>Only verified Stripe subscription state changes paid entitlement.</li>
<li>Duplicate valid webhook events are safely idempotent, while invalid signatures are rejected before state mutation.</li>
<li>Monthly usage rollups can be rerun without duplicating summaries.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Limitations and scope</h2>
<p>The public demo simulates AI usage and uses Stripe test mode only. Deliberate non-goals include real AI-provider calls, real invoices or collections, production Stripe, production identity, Kubernetes and paid infrastructure. The Render free-tier service may also take about a minute to wake after inactivity.</p>

<h2>Practical relevance</h2>
<p>The design addresses common commercial failure modes in usage-based products: duplicate metering, inconsistent quota enforcement, forged subscription events and unreconciled monthly usage.</p>

<h2>What this demonstrates</h2>
<p>Backend/API engineering, tenancy boundaries, idempotency, quota semantics, signed third-party events, reproducible testing and clear separation between usage estimates, entitlements and real billing.</p>

